{"name":"Cherokee Dignity","hex":"#dd7722","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dignity","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The state of being dignified or worthy of esteem: elevation of mind or character.","Decorum, formality, stateliness. (Discuss this sense)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dignity"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cherokee","blocks":[{"text":"The Cherokee or Tsalagi people are one of the Indigenous peoples of the Southeastern Woodlands of the United States. Prior to the 18th century, they were concentrated in their ancestral homelands, living in towns along river valleys in what is now southwestern North Carolina, southeastern Tennessee, southwestern Virginia, parts of western South Carolina, northern Georgia, and northeastern Alabama, with hunting grounds extending into Kentucky. Together, these lands encompassed approximately 40,000 square miles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Henry_Inman_-_Sequoyah_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/330px-Henry_Inman_-_Sequoyah_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cherokee"},{"title":"Dignity","blocks":[{"text":"Dignity is the right of a person to be valued and respected for their own sake, and to be treated ethically. In this context, it is of significance in morality, ethics, law and politics as an extension of the Enlightenment-era concepts of inherent, inalienable rights. The term may also be used to describe personal conduct, as in \"behaving with dignity\"."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dignity"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mound_Builder_City.jpeg/500px-Mound_Builder_City.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mound_Builder_City.jpeg/500px-Mound_Builder_City.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMound_Builder_City.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg/500px-Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg/1280px-Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg/500px-Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Great Smoky Mountains","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASmoky_Mtn_View.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg/500px-Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg/1280px-Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg/500px-Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An annotated copy of a hand-painted Catawba deerskin map of the tribes between Charleston (left) and Virginia (right) following the displacements of a century of disease and slavery among the indigenous peoples of the Americas and the 1715–7 Yamasee War. The Cherokee are labelled as \"Cherrikies\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Three_Cherokee.jpg/500px-Three_Cherokee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.o